    {
      "id": "9-math-4-ex5.1-q1",
      "questionNumber": 1,
      "question": "Draw $\\triangle ABC$ with $AB = 5$ cm, $\\angle A = 70°$ and $\\angle B = 60°$. Draw the circumcircle of $\\triangle ABC$. Is the centre inside or outside the triangle?",
      "solution": [
        {
          "step": 1,
          "content": "**Find the third angle:** $$\\angle C = 180° - 70° - 60° = 50°$$"
        },
        {
          "step": 2,
          "content": "**Draw the triangle:** Draw $AB = 5$ cm. At $A$ draw a ray making $70°$ with $AB$, and at $B$ draw a ray making $60°$ with $AB$. The two rays meet at $C$."
        },
        {
          "step": 3,
          "content": "**Draw the circumcircle:** Draw the perpendicular bisectors of any two sides (say $AB$ and $BC$). Their point of intersection is the circumcentre $O$. With $O$ as centre and radius $OA$, draw the circle — it passes through $A$, $B$ and $C$."
        },
        {
          "step": 4,
          "content": "**Conclusion:** All three angles ($70°$, $60°$, $50°$) are less than $90°$, so $\\triangle ABC$ is an acute-angled triangle. **The circumcentre lies inside the triangle.**"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "The circumcentre lies inside the triangle (all angles are acute).",
      "verified": true,
      "notes": "**Exam Tip:** For an acute-angled triangle the circumcentre is inside; for an obtuse-angled triangle it is outside; for a right-angled triangle it lies at the midpoint of the hypotenuse.",
